description:
An elevation filter with only a MINIMUM (or only a maximum) set does not filter. src/filter.ts:384-387:

    } else if (f.elevMin !== null) {
      occurrenceClauses.push(`(elevation_m IS NULL OR elevation_m >= ${f.elevMin})`);
    } else if (f.elevMax !== null) {
      occurrenceClauses.push(`(elevation_m IS NULL OR elevation_m <= ${f.elevMax})`);
    }

Because elevation_m is populated on only 44,218 of 98,775 rows (45%) — it comes solely from the Ecdysis arm; int_combined.sql hardcodes NULL::INTEGER for the other four arms (lines 115, 167, 255, 319) — the IS NULL disjunct admits every iNat observation (28,850) and every checklist row (19,929) in the state, regardless of the bound.

MEASURED, on public/data/occurrences.db:
  "Bombus above 1700m" (elevMin=1700, elevMax=null)
    current behavior ............ 26 species
    no elevation filter at all .. 26 species   <- identical
    NULL excluded ............... 12 species
  Bombus records with a measured elevation >= 1700m: 48
  Bombus records passing only because elevation is NULL: 19,156

So a user who sets a minimum sees exactly what they would have seen with no filter, and reasonably concludes the 26 species have been recorded above 1700m. Note the inconsistency: when BOTH bounds are set, line 383 uses elevation_m IS NOT NULL AND ... BETWEEN, which excludes NULLs. The permissive branch applies only to one-sided bounds, so the same control changes meaning depending on how many boxes are filled.

The behavior may have been a deliberate "don't hide records we can't place" choice for map plotting. It is at minimum undocumented and unlabelled, and it is wrong for any read of the result as a presence claim.

FIX OPTIONS (decide before building)
  a) Make the bound always imply elevation_m IS NOT NULL, matching the two-sided branch. One rule everywhere. Changes what existing shared /?elevMin=... links display.
  b) Keep permissive plotting but label it in the filter UI ("N records have no elevation data and are shown regardless").
  c) Leave the map alone and diverge per-surface — this is what the taxa-pane bead does under its D-02, which is a workaround for this defect, not a fix for it.

RELATED: DEM elevation backfill would shrink the NULL population from 54,557 to ~19,929 (checklist only, which must not be backfilled) and make whichever option is chosen far less lossy.
